 equal priorities leave in an unspecified order. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"min"},{"type":"text","text":" changes only when a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"strictly smaller priority arrives, or when the minimum leaves or its priority increases. To"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"break ties a particular way, put the rule in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Priority"},{"type":"text","text":", for example "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"(distance, index)"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Priorities must not be NaN. Not a "},{"code":"Sequence","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", as swift-collections’ "},{"code":"Heap","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is not:"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"unordered","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" lists the contents. Storage is two arrays, copied on write."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Use it when items are dense indices whose priorities must be lowered in place (Dijkstra, Prim,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"A*). "},{"code":"Heap","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" has no way to find or change a queued element, so it can only stand in by pushing"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"duplicates and skipping stale ones: in Dijkstra that costs 1.1–1.4× (the gap narrows as graphs"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"grow, and with few distinct weights), and about 2.6× when every item is decreased once. For pushes and pops alone, "},{"code":"Heap","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"better structure: it stores bare elements, and sorts 10⁶ "},{"code":"Int","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"s in about 0.7× the time."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"An index that has left the queue is indistinguishable from one